---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="How a photo gets on screen — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="What actually happens between clicking a photo and seeing it, and all the work LightPhotos skips to make that feel instant."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-07-08">July 8, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>How a photo gets on screen</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          What actually happens between clicking a photo and seeing it, and all
          the work the app skips to make that feel instant.
        </p>
      </header>

      <p>
        I want LightPhotos to feel instant. You open a folder of holiday photos,
        the thumbnails fill in, you double-click one and it opens full size, you
        scroll the wheel and it zooms. None of that should surprise anyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        What surprised me is how much work the app has to skip to make it feel
        that ordinary. Here is everything that happens to one photo on its way
        to your screen.
      </p>

      <h2>Three different jobs</h2>
      <p>
        A photo takes one of three paths through the app, and I treat them as
        three separate problems.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Opening.</strong> You clicked, and you want the photo large on
          screen right now.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Browsing.</strong> The grid needs a small version of every
          photo you can currently see.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Exporting.</strong> You want a JPEG on disk with your edits
          applied.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Opening is about the gap between your click and something appearing.
        Browsing is about doing one small job a hundred times without falling
        over. Exporting is about getting the pixels exactly right, and it can
        take as long as it likes, because you already pressed the button and
        walked away.
      </p>

      <h2>Opening a photo</h2>
      <p>
        A camera RAW file is not an image. It is the raw readout from the
        sensor, and turning it into something you can look at takes real
        computation. On my machine a Sony ARW takes about 250 ms.
      </p>
      <p>
        Almost every RAW file comes with a shortcut, though. Your camera already
        wrote a small JPEG preview so it could show you the shot on its own
        screen. Pulling that out takes about 35 ms, so that is what I show you
        first. The full decode keeps running in the background and swaps in when
        it is ready.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 196" role="img" aria-label="Timeline: pass one puts an embedded preview on screen at 35 milliseconds, while pass two finishes the full sensor decode at 250 milliseconds and swaps in.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">OPENING A CAMERA RAW FILE</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="50">Pass 1</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="68">embedded preview</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="176" y="36" width="60" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="248" y="56">35 ms &middot; on screen</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="116">Pass 2</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="134">full sensor decode</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="176" y="102" width="431" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="192" y="122">250 ms &middot; sharp version swaps in</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="236" y1="28" x2="236" y2="158" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>

          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="176" y1="158" x2="624" y2="158"></line>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="176" y1="158" x2="176" y2="164"></line>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="236" y1="158" x2="236" y2="164"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="176" y="180">click</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="248" y="180">the only wait you actually notice ends here</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Nothing here got faster. The app just found something worth showing
          while the expensive work was still running.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        A plain JPEG has no embedded preview and does not need one. Decoding a
        JPEG at a small size is already quick, so the first pass does the whole
        job and the second pass never runs. Same code path, no special case.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is a third pass for when you zoom in past the point where the
        screen-sized version still holds detail. Full-resolution decodes cost
        seconds and hundreds of megabytes, so I only run them when you actually
        zoom in that far.
      </p>

      <h2>After that, it lives on the GPU</h2>
      <p>
        Once a photo is decoded, it gets uploaded to the graphics card once.
        Zooming and panning never touch the image data again. They change a
        handful of numbers that get sent to the card each frame.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        Zooming does not touch the photo at all. It changes four numbers.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        The editing sliders work the same way. Drag Exposure and the new value
        goes to the GPU, where the shader recomputes the picture on the fly. No
        re-decode, no pixel loop on the CPU. You are looking at the original
        decode with some arithmetic applied on the way to the screen.
      </p>

      <h2>Two renderers</h2>
      <p>
        The buttons, grid, filmstrip and sliders are drawn by egui, an
        immediate-mode UI library. The photo itself is drawn by a few hundred
        lines of hand-written GPU code, sitting underneath a transparent panel.
      </p>
      <p>
        Splitting it this way lets me write the interface in the loose,
        convenient style egui encourages, while the part that has to be fast
        about pixels stays small enough to hand-tune. A general-purpose toolkit
        would have made the photo a widget, and widgets do not accept a
        24-megapixel texture with a custom shader without a fight.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the four builds meet</h2>
      <p>
        macOS, Linux, Windows and the browser build differ in exactly one place,
        which is how they turn a file into pixels. After that they all converge.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 280" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: four platform builds fork only at the decode layer, then converge into one shared edit pipeline feeding both the GPU tone shader and the exporter.">
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="70" y="20" text-anchor="middle">macOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="160" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="230" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Linux</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="320" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="390" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Windows</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="480" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="550" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Browser</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M70 30 V52 M230 30 V52 M390 30 V52 M550 30 V52"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="52" width="140" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="70" y="73" text-anchor="middle">ImageIO</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="160" y="52" width="300" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="310" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Rust decoders</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="480" y="52" width="140" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="550" y="73" text-anchor="middle">same, in Web Workers</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M70 86 V100 H550 M550 86 V100 M310 86 V152"></path>
          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="0" y1="116" x2="640" y2="116" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="136">THE ONLY FORK &mdash; EVERYTHING BELOW IS SHARED</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M304 152 L316 152 L310 162 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="90" y="166" width="460" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="320" y="187" text-anchor="middle">One edit function, in linear light</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M200 200 V216 H440 M440 200 V216 M200 216 V230 M440 216 V230"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M194 230 L206 230 L200 240 Z M434 230 L446 230 L440 240 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="90" y="244" width="220" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="200" y="265" text-anchor="middle">Tone shader on the GPU</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="330" y="244" width="220" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="440" y="265" text-anchor="middle">Exported JPEG</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Fork low, converge high. Every layer above the fork is a layer that
          cannot drift between platforms.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The GPU tone shader is a direct translation of that one edit function.
        The function that bakes edits into finished pixels has exactly two
        callers, the exporter and the thumbnail you are looking at.
      </p>
      <p>
        That second fact is the whole reason an exported JPEG matches what you
        saw on screen. I never built that as a feature. It falls out of there
        being one code path.
      </p>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Ask what you can show before the real work finishes.</strong>
          Cheap then correct is what turns a 250 ms wait into a 35 ms one
          without making anything faster.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Upload once, then transform cheaply.</strong> If an action
          repeats 60 times a second, it should change a few numbers, never
          re-run the expensive step.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Treat different waits as different problems.</strong> Opening,
          browsing and exporting look like one job called "decode a photo". One
          shared path would have served none of them well.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fork low, converge high.</strong> Platform differences belong
          as close to the operating system as you can push them.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        If you want to see how this feels, there are builds for all four
        platforms on the <a href="/downloads.html">downloads page</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
